# Tech design document skeleton

Use it to create a feature's `{{vars.techDesignFile}}` or the project baseline, and to decide which
section a requirement belongs to. Sections 3 to 7 follow their section guides.

## Rules

- Keep the section numbers; leave out a section without applicable content entirely (no empty
  heading, no "N/A" text: verdicts live in the brief). Section 8 appears once an ID is retired.
- Every requirement, constraint, decision and entity is defined in exactly one file and one
  section; elsewhere cite its ID. Rows that hold for every feature belong to the baseline.
- Feature sections 6 and 7 hold only what differs from the baseline; a refinement cites the
  `SYS-` ID it refines.
- Long-lived content only: no ticket keys, request text, people, dates, task progress, open
  questions, follow-ups or screening verdicts (those live in the brief).
- Write in the language of the requirement files; IDs, codes and units stay as they are.

## Where requirements go

| Section | Holds | Codes (sub-characteristics) | Also called |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 Context and constraints | the technical context, `CON-n`, `DEC-n` | none | constraints, decisions |
| 2 Quality requirements and trade-offs | end-to-end targets of the feature, Trade-offs | FUNC, PERF, REL (faultlessness, fault tolerance), SEC, SAFE, COMPL | software architecture quality attributes |
| 3 Data | logical entities, data requirements | DCONS, PRIV, one-entity rows of other codes | data structure, storage |
| 4 Interfaces and communication | interface inventory, per-interface requirements | COMP (interoperability), FLEX (replaceability), per-interface PERF, REL, SEC, DCONS | communication patterns, integrations |
| 5 UI appearance | placement rows, strict rows under `ui-design` | IC, SAFE (hazard warning), FLEX (adaptability) | UI, UX, usability |
| 6 Deployment and operations | baseline deltas | REL (availability, recoverability), FLEX (scalability, installability), COMP (co-existence), OBS, DEPLOY, COST | infrastructure and operations |
| 7 Engineering | baseline deltas | MAINT, DX, CICD | code quality and organization, CI/CD |
| 8 Retired IDs | IDs no longer in force | any | |

When two sections fit, the narrower one wins: a requirement of one interface goes to 4, of one
entity to 3, of the whole feature to 2.

## Feature document

`{{vars.requirementsDir}}/<feature>/{{vars.techDesignFile}}`:

```markdown
# <Feature name>: technical requirements

Prefix: CHK · Business rules: [{{vars.requirementsFile}}]({{vars.requirementsFile}}) · Baseline: [{{vars.techDesignFile}}](../{{vars.techDesignFile}})

## 1. Context and constraints

<2 to 4 sentences: what the feature does technically and which systems or features it touches.>

| ID | Constraint | Why it binds |
|---|---|---|
| CHK-CON-1 | Orders live in the shop database, whose schema the platform team owns | existing system of record |

### Decisions

**CHK-DEC-1 Exports are produced in the background.**
- Context: exports of up to 100 000 Orders take longer than a request may last (CHK-PERF-1).
- Decision: an export runs as a background job; the Customer is notified when it is ready (CHK-4.2).
- Alternatives: streaming in the request (rejected: proxy limits); nightly files (rejected: stale).
- Consequences: export status becomes stored state (section 3); failures need an alert (section 6).

## 2. Quality requirements and trade-offs

| ID | Sub-characteristic | Requirement | Measure | Verify by | Priority | Source |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

Trade-offs:
- CHK-PERF-1 over CHK-COST-1: <what gives way, and why the user chose it>.

## 3. Data

### <Term>
### Data requirements

## 4. Interfaces and communication

### Interface inventory
### Interface requirements

## 5. UI appearance

### Strict UI behaviour

## 6. Deployment and operations

## 7. Engineering

## 8. Retired IDs

| ID | Was | Replaced by | Reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| CHK-REL-1 | the Customer restarts a failed export | CHK-REL-4 | background exports retry by themselves (CHK-DEC-1) |
```

Sections 2, 5, 6 and 7 hold one requirement table each, in the requirement format; `### Strict UI
behaviour` appears only under `ui-design`.

## Project baseline

`{{vars.requirementsDir}}/{{vars.techDesignFile}}`, created with the user's consent the first time a
task needs a project-wide item. Same sections and rules, prefix `SYS`:

```markdown
# Project technical requirements

Prefix: SYS · Features: [README.md](README.md)

## 1. Context and constraints
Runtime and hosting, data store, users and scale, sensitive data, CI host: SYS-CON-n, SYS-DEC-n.
## 2. Quality requirements and trade-offs
Targets every feature inherits: SYS-SEC-1 every write is authorized on the server side.
## 3. Data
Conventions every entity follows: identity, time zones, money, default retention.
## 4. Interfaces and communication
API style, versioning and deprecation, error outcomes, idempotency of writes.
## 5. UI appearance
Accessibility level, supported platforms and browsers, smallest viewport.
## 6. Deployment and operations
Environments, deployment units, rollout and rollback, availability, backups, observability, cost.
## 7. Engineering
Module boundaries, test levels, CI gates, local setup.
## 8. Retired IDs
```

The baseline holds no feature's own rows. A code that a `SYS-` row fully covers for a feature is
screened as Baseline and gets no feature row.
